= Abstract

#pendiente[Provisional translation of the Resumen. Keep both versions in sync.]

This project focuses on the design and implementation of a data-acquisition system to characterize a variable-gain actuator based on a capstan drive mechanism for robotics. The actuator drives a lever-type arm whose mechanical behavior is analogous to a human bicep performing curls: the force required for actuation is maximum when the arm is fully flexed and decreases as it extends.

A dedicated PCB is designed and manufactured to acquire force, angular position and motor current measurements; the mechanical design required to integrate the measurement system with the actuator is carried out; and real-time acquisition and logging firmware is developed. Systematic measurement campaigns characterize the relationship between arm position, mechanism gain and actuation force, validating the variable-gain behavior of the system.

*Keywords:* data acquisition, variable-gain actuator, capstan drive, instrumentation, PCB design, force sensing, bioinspired robotics.
